    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u explain how a diesel turbocharger actually works? specifically how the exhaust gas turns into boost in the intake? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A diesel turbocharger uses high-pressure exhaust gases from the engine's combustion cycle to spin a turbine connected to the exhaust side. This same turbine shaft drives a compressor wheel on the intake side, forcing ambient air into the engine manifold under pressure. Crucially, this mechanism relies purely on the energy of the exhaust flow rather than the Turbo's behavior; if the exhaust stops, the turbine slows and so does the compressor, causing the system to revert to a wastegate's default state. In modern diesels, an intercooler cools this compressed air before it enters the cylinders, increasing efficiency and power output without additional fuel.",
      "source": "target",
